\section{Vintages}{

\code{admin_vintage} selects which administrative units exist:
\itemize{
\item \code{"2025"} — 3259 freguesias, the current set (Lei 25-A/2025, in force from
the 2025 autárquicas, which restored units aggregated in 2013).
\item \code{"2013"} — 3092 freguesias (Lei 11-A/2013). \strong{This is what INE still
publishes}, including on indicators labelled "NUTS - 2024".
\item \code{"2011"} — 4260 freguesias, the map the 2011 census used, in force from
2004 until the 2013 reform.
\item \code{"2001"} — 4241 freguesias, the map the 2001 census used. 22 freguesias
were created and 3 dissolved between the two censuses.
}

The pre-2013 maps are named after the census that recorded them, because
neither was made by a single law. The 1991 census used an earlier map again
(4208 freguesias in 305 municípios, before Odivelas, Trofa and Vizela were
created in 1998), which geopt does not have.

\code{nuts_vintage} selects the NUTS revision: \code{"2024"} (9 NUTS II, 26 NUTS III),
\code{"2013"} (7 / 25) or \code{"2002"} (7 / 30).

\code{health_vintage} selects the health geography: \code{"2024"} (regiões de saúde +
ULS) or \code{"2022"} (the former ARS + ACES). Health columns are omitted unless
asked for, or unless \code{level} is a health level: those read the geography
their units belong to, \code{"2024"} for \code{"reg_saude"}, \code{"uls"} and
\code{"uls_municipio"}, and \code{"2022"} for \code{"aces"} and \code{"aces_municipio"}.
}

\section{Codes are vintage-local}{

A DTMNFR identifies a unit \emph{within its own vintage only}. None of the 302
freguesias restored in 2025 reused its pre-2013 code, so comparing a code
across vintages — or joining data of one vintage onto codes of another —
silently mismatches. Use \code{\link[=pt_crosswalk]{pt_crosswalk()}} to convert, and \code{\link[=pt_recode]{pt_recode()}} to
carry values across.
}

